+## Venus E Mini (VNSEM)
+
+The Venus E Mini talks to the CT a little differently from a Venus E 2.0 or 3.0:
+
+- It always asks for the CT as `000000000000`, even once it is paired, and
+  pairs only when the answer carries the CT's own MAC. AstraMeter answers it
+  that way, also with `CT_MAC` set.
+- It never reports a phase (always phase `0`), so the dashboard shows it as
+  *unassigned*. That is normal for a mini, also on a real CT003.
+- Instead of its power it reports a status: `1` while it is working, `0` while
+  idle.
+- It steers itself on the grid value it receives, divided by the number of
+  batteries the CT tells it share the grid.
+
+Use `DEVICE_TYPE = ct003` with a mini: it expects the CT003's 28-field answer.
+
+### How AstraMeter steers a mini
+
+| Setup | What the mini does |
+|---|---|
+| Only minis, no sensors | Exactly what a real CT003 does: every working mini takes an equal share (1/N) of the grid. |
+| Minis next to a Venus (or another battery that reports its power) | Each mini without a sensor takes 1/N of the grid, where N counts all batteries. The balancer spreads the rest over the batteries it can measure. |
+| A mini with a [battery power sensor](#battery-power-sensors) | Steered like a Venus: it gets its own target on its configured phase, with fair distribution, weights, pacing and saturation detection. |
+| Any mix of the above | Each mini follows its own row. Minis with a sensor count as measured batteries. |
+
+A mini without a sensor is invisible to the balancer, so next to a Venus
+AstraMeter adds two safeguards:
+
+- **No cross-charging.** The mini is never shown export while every measured
+  battery is discharging, nor import while they are all charging. Without this,
+  the mini could charge from the Venus, or the other way round.
+- **Paced.** The value it receives changes by at most 300 W × N per answer, so
+  it doesn't jump ahead of a Venus that ramps more slowly.
+
+Its limits:
+
+- Fair distribution, distribution weights and minimum-efficient-power can't
+  include it, since its power is unknown.
+- AstraMeter can't see when it is full or empty. An empty mini keeps its 1/N.
+  That part of the grid stays uncovered at first, and the other batteries take
+  it over in the following seconds, more slowly than when a battery's
+  saturation is detected.
+
+A battery power sensor removes all of these limits.
+
+## Battery power sensors
+
+A battery power sensor tells AstraMeter what a battery that doesn't report its
+power is actually doing. A smart plug in front of the battery (a HomeWizard
+Energy Socket, a Shelly Plug, …), a Home Assistant sensor, or anything else
+AstraMeter can read as a power meter will do. With one, the battery is steered
+like any other. When the sensor gives no reading for 5 seconds, the battery
+falls back to the behaviour it has without one.
+
+Requirements:
+
+- It reports watts and updates about once a second. Slower readings make the
+  balancing lag behind.
+- It counts power the battery **draws** (charging) as positive, which is how a
+  plug in front of the battery measures it. If yours counts discharging as
+  positive, put a `-` before the source.
+- It needs `ACTIVE_CONTROL` (the default).
+
+### config.ini
+
+Under `[CT003]` (or `[CT002]`):
+
+- **BATTERY_POWER_SENSORS** — `<battery mac>=[-]<source>[@<phase>]`, comma
+  separated, one entry per battery.
+  - `<battery mac>` is the MAC the battery polls with (12 hex digits, colons
+    allowed). It is shown on the dashboard and in the log.
+  - `<source>` is either the name of a section in the same file, or a Home
+    Assistant entity id (anything with a dot, such as `sensor.mini_power`).
+  - `<phase>` is `A`, `B`, `C` or `D` (combined): the phase the battery is
+    steered on, since a mini reports none. Default `A`.
+- **BATTERY_SENSORS_HA_URL** / **BATTERY_SENSORS_HA_TOKEN** — where to read Home
+  Assistant entities from (URL such as `http://192.168.1.10:8123`, and a
+  long-lived access token). Not needed inside the Home Assistant add-on, which
+  reads them through the Supervisor.
+
+A source section has a `SOURCE` key naming a power meter type (`JSON_HTTP`,
+`SHELLY`, `TASMOTA`, `MQTT`, `HOMEASSISTANT`, `HOMEWIZARD`, …), plus that
+type's usual keys from [Powermeter sources](powermeters.md). Its name must not
+start with a meter type (`SHELLY_…`, `TASMOTA_…`), otherwise it would also be
+read as a grid meter.
+
+```ini
+[CT003]
+BATTERY_POWER_SENSORS = ccc837b39dbe=BATTERY_MINI_PLUG@A, aabbccddeeff=sensor.mini2_power@B
+# Only for the Home Assistant entity, outside the add-on:
+BATTERY_SENSORS_HA_URL = http://192.168.1.10:8123
+BATTERY_SENSORS_HA_TOKEN = <long-lived access token>
+
+# A HomeWizard Energy Socket in front of the first mini (Local API enabled).
+[BATTERY_MINI_PLUG]
+SOURCE = JSON_HTTP
+URL = http://192.168.1.50/api/v1/data
+JSON_PATHS = $.active_power_w
+```
+
+A Shelly plug works the same way:
+
+```ini
+[BATTERY_MINI_SHELLY]
+SOURCE = SHELLY
+TYPE = PLUGS
+IP = 192.168.1.51
+```
+
+### Home Assistant add-on
+
+Set **Battery Power Sensors** on the Configuration tab, using entities only:
+`ccc837b39dbe=sensor.mini_plug_power@A`. Several minis go comma separated.
+For a section source, use a custom config file instead.
+
+### ESPHome
+
+Any ESPHome sensor works, so the ESP32 needs no Home Assistant for it. See
+[installation/esphome.md](installation/esphome.md#battery-power-sensors).
+
+### Checking it works
+
+At startup the log shows `Battery <mac>: reading its power from <source>`,
+followed by `power from <source> is live` once the first reading arrives. On the
+dashboard the mini then gets a phase and a real power instead of *unassigned*.
+If the source fails, the log says `<source> unusable (...)` and the mini falls
+back to 1/N.
+
